# Line endings.
#
# The repo is written on Windows and deployed to Linux, which is exactly the
# situation that produces the classic `bash: /r: No such file or directory` —
# a shell script checked out with CRLF, whose shebang line then ends in a
# carriage return the kernel treats as part of the interpreter's name. The
# error names a file nobody wrote and is genuinely baffling the first time.
#
# So: anything the server executes or reads as configuration keeps LF on every
# platform, whatever a developer's core.autocrlf says.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
Caddyfile text eol=lf
deploy/Caddyfile text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
